+**Account write serialization (review F1):** users carry separate authGeneration and writeFence counters. authGeneration changes on suspension/deletion/privilege changes; writeFence is incremented by every guarded transaction, so ordinary activity does not invalidate an asynchronous job's captured authorization generation. Every transaction that creates or modifies a contribution, personal record, moderation action, session, upload reservation or export must conditionally update the active actor's fence; transactions writing private care data also fence its owning reporter. Account suspension/deletion/role changes update the same document, authGeneration and writeFence. Initial registration atomically creates the active user/fence after verified Facebook identity; it cannot bypass an existing deleting account. The Mongo session-store set/touch implementation must participate in these guarded writes, rather than allowing a generic store to recreate sessions after erasure. A mere preceding read is insufficient: a write conflict retries the whole transaction and reevaluates status; no active-account match aborts it. Cleanup jobs use a separate narrowly scoped state-checked system path that can erase data for deleting accounts but cannot publish new content. This applies to place/feeding creation and edits, not only claims.
+
+Asynchronous image/export jobs reserve a durable operation ID, immutable object key, account generation, resource version and bounded lease BEFORE external work. Finalization rechecks/fences all relevant accounts and the parent resource in one transaction; cancelled, deleted, hidden or generation-changed work cannot become ready. Failed finalization schedules retirement of the exact staged object. Deletion cancels reservations, increments the account generation and records reserved object keys in its off-VM ledger; preserve cancellation/cleanup records until work has stopped or its bounded lease and external-request deadline have elapsed and those keys are reconciled. Only then may the erasure job report complete. Physical retirement failures remain pending and alert; an upload cannot recreate a ready reference after erasure.
+
 Do not add denormalized place recency in v1: derive latest visible feeding by `occurredAt,_id` using indexed per-place lookups after bounding the place query. This avoids stale recency after backdating, moderation and deletion. Aggregate only anonymous band/count summaries and currently active care counts. Benchmark realistic datasets before changing to caches; a future materialized cache must update transactionally and be reconstructible.
 
 **Feeding time:** `occurredAt` defaults to server now, allowed back seven days; reject >5 minutes into future and clamp accepted small clock skew to server now. `createdAt` is always server-generated. Timestamp changes are explicit edits with an audit record. Public history exposes only Europe/Stockholm calendar date and server-derived recency category/day label; exact occurredAt/createdAt, hidden sort values and actor identifiers stay private, including in cursors and IDs. Own history can show precise time. Public status changes can still reveal that activity happened recently; coarsening is not anonymity proof. Invalid “future feeding” must not keep a place green indefinitely.
